1 Symbol_Assign

   Gives a name, a DCL symbol, to an integer or a string.

   Format

     symbol-name =[=] expression
     symbol-name :=[=] string

   "=" and "==" give the symbol an expression's value (see =), ":=" and
   ":==" a string, the rest of the line (see :=).  One "=" or ":=" makes
   a local symbol, and "==" or ":==" a global one.

2 Parameters

 symbol-name

   A name of letters, digits, "$", and "_", starting with a letter, "$",
   or "_".  An "*" in the name says how short an abbreviation still
   means the symbol: after FO*RTH :== $FORTH, FO, FOR, FORT, and FORTH
   all do.

 expression

   A DCL expression (see Expressions).

 string

   The rest of the line: outside quotes, letters are made uppercase,
   runs of blanks become one, and a "!" begins a comment; quotes keep
   text as written, and are removed ("" inside them is a quote).

2 Description

   When the first word of a command is a symbol, its value replaces the
   word before the command is read.  A value beginning with "$" makes
   the symbol a foreign command (see FOREIGN); any other value is the
   start of a command, so a symbol can abbreviate or rename one.  To use
   a symbol's value elsewhere in a command, put it between apostrophes
   (see Substitution).

   Local symbols belong to the current command level: the terminal's,
   or the command procedure's (see @), and go when it ends.  A local
   symbol is found before a global one of the same name.  SHOW SYMBOL
   displays them, and DELETE/SYMBOL deletes them.  These are DCL
   symbols, not the machine's symbol table, which ASM and the debugger
   use (see the debugger's HELP SHOW SYMBOL).

2 Expressions

   The value of an "=" or "==" assignment, and of IF's, WRITE's, and
   EXIT's expressions, is a DCL expression.  Its operands are:

     integers     13, -5, %X1F (hexadecimal), %O17 (octal), %D10
     strings      "in quotes", with "" for a quote inside
     symbols      a name, replaced by the symbol's value
     functions    F$LENGTH(...) and the other lexical functions (see
                  Lexicals)

   The operators, from the first done to the last; parentheses group:

     + -          unary plus and minus
     * /          multiplication and division (integers; no fraction)
     + -          addition and subtraction; on two strings,
                  concatenation and removing the first occurrence
     .EQ. .NE. .LT. .LE. .GT. .GE.         compare numbers
     .EQS. .NES. .LTS. .LES. .GTS. .GES.   compare strings
     .NOT.        inverts every bit
     .AND.        bitwise and
     .OR.         bitwise or

   A comparison is 1 when true and 0 when false.  Where an integer is
   needed, a string that holds a number is that number, one beginning
   with T or Y (either case) is 1, and any other is 0.  Where a string
   is needed, an integer is its decimal digits.  Integers are 32 bits
   and wrap around.  In a test, odd is true and even false.

   Examples:

     $ BALANCE = 142 * 14
     $ WEEKEND = "Saturday, " + "Sunday"
     $ BIG = COUNT .GT. 10 .AND. NAME .EQS. "TOTAL"

2 Status

   Each command ends with a status, a VMS condition value, which DCL
   keeps in the global symbol $STATUS, and its severity in $SEVERITY:

     $ SHOW SYMBOL $STATUS
       $STATUS == "%X00030001"

   The low three bits are the severity: 0 a warning, 1 success, 2 an
   error, 3 informational, 4 fatal.  So $STATUS is odd (true) after a
   command that succeeded:

     $ IF $STATUS THEN WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "it worked"

   Bit 28 set means the status's message has been shown already, so it
   isn't shown again.  A command that succeeds gives %X00030001; one
   that fails gives VMS's condition value for its message, where VMS
   has one.  RUN gives the image's exit status (showing its message if
   it failed), and @ the procedure's (see EXIT).  SHOW SYMBOL, IF, and
   CONTINUE leave $STATUS as it was when they succeed.  In a command
   procedure, the status decides whether the procedure goes on (see
   ON).

2 Substitution

   A symbol's value takes the place of its name in a command when the
   name is between apostrophes:

     $ TYPE 'FILE'
     $ @NEXT 'P1' 'P2'

   Inside quotes, the name takes two apostrophes before it and one
   after: "Creating ''NAME'.DAT".  A lexical function can be between
   apostrophes too: 'F$LENGTH(X)'.  Outside quotes, the closing
   apostrophe may be left out at the end of a word ('NAME), and a value
   that itself has a symbol between apostrophes is replaced in turn.  An
   undefined symbol is replaced by nothing.  This happens before
   anything else reads the command, so the value can be any part of it,
   the verb included.  The value is then made uppercase with the rest of
   the command, unless it's inside quotes.

   &NAME is also replaced by the symbol's value, later: after the
   command's first word is looked up as a symbol, and after the command
   is made uppercase, so the value keeps its case, and it stays one
   parameter even with blanks in it.  It must follow a blank or a
   special character, and does nothing inside quotes, in a ":="
   assignment, or in an @ command's parameters.

     $ COUNT = 1
     $ TYPE &P'COUNT'          ! 'COUNT' first, giving &P1

   Data lines in a procedure, and debugger commands, are not
   substituted.

2 Examples

   1.$ SD :== SET DEFAULT

     Makes SD a command that sets the default directory.

   2.$ FORTH :== $DUA1:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE

     Makes FORTH a foreign command.

   3.$ GREETING == "Hello, ""World"""

     Makes GREETING a global symbol whose value is Hello, "World".

   4.$ TOTAL = COUNT + 1

     Makes TOTAL a local symbol, one more than COUNT.
